  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nSingle band of lake effect showers stretches down the full\nlength of Lake Michigan early this afternoon. Showers in our\narea have been largely confined to northwest Indiana. After some\nwet snow mixed in with the rain showers this morning over\nportions of Lake County Indiana, precipitation has transitioned\nto all rain. No accumulations were reported to our office,\nthough on webcams we did see some brief slushy accumulations\nappearing to be less than an inch on grassy and elevated \nsurfaces this morning before it quickly melted. \n\nWater vapor imagery early this afternoon depicts one last well\ndefined shortwave trough and associated vort max over eastern \nWI. This trough axis will move south of the Lake by early this\nevening which should result in lowering inversion heights\ntonight and Saturday. Inversion heights this evening should\nremain favorable for a formidable lake effect band. High \nresolution guidance does suggest that this band could wobble \nback west into northeast IL this evening, likely in response to \na bit of a mesolow developing over the lake. The weakening \nboundary layer flow should promote a strong land breeze this \nevening and give still favorable instability, certainly \nplausible that a meso-low could form. If a meso-low doesn't \nform, then lake effect should remain targeted on northwest \nIndiana this evening and regardless of meso-low development or \nno meso-low the lake effect should gradually shift eastward \novernight into Saturday morning focusing more into Porter County\nand eventually east of our CWA Saturday.\n\nThe lowering inversion heights, less favorable stability \nprofiles over land, and weakening onshore boundary layer flow \nwould all suggest this lake effect band should struggle to \npenetrate as far inland later this evening and especially \novernight into Saturday. Thermodynamic profiles would \npotentially support some wet snow mixing in this evening, given \nsufficient inland penetration, but with sfc temps likely just \nabove freezing, even if the band were to penetrate far enough \ninland where the air mass would be cold enough for snow, \naccumulations would be unlikely. Overnight the lesser inland \npenetration should reduce snow chances, as too will the \ninversion heights which should drop below the -10C level, \nleaving lake effect convection lacking the ice nuclei needed for\nsnow. \n\nOutside of the lack effect tomorrow, the remainder of the area\nwill become sunny. Mostly sunny skies are then expected through\nthe first half of the upcoming work week. Temperatures this\nweekend will be near average (though given the unseasonable\nwarmth of late, will likely feel pretty cool). Generally dry\nweather is expected for the upcoming work week with highs\nwarming back into the 50s and possibly into the 60s by the end\nof the week. Normal highs are in the 40s this weekend through\nnext week. \n\n- Izzi",
